Viral Conjunctivitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Watery discharge, Conjunctival hyperemia, Preauricular lymphadenopathy

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-048
Reason for Visit: Acute "pink eye" with severe grittiness and vision blurred by discharge.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute, bilateral onset of severe ocular redness, profuse watery discharge, and a distressing "gritty" sensation. The patient reports blurred vision and significant eyelid edema. An urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform a thorough slit-lamp equivalent evaluation (using high-power magnification/fluorescein staining), definitively rule out sight-threatening bacterial keratitis or acute angle-closure glaucoma, and establish immediate infection control. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of cross-contamination or missing a serious ocular emergency, satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My eyes are bright red, they feel like they're full of sand, and I can barely see through the constant watering.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 24 hours ago in the left eye and rapidly spread to the right. The patient reports bilateral watery ocular discharge and diffuse conjunctival hyperemia. Describes an intense gritty or sandy sensation. Notes significant morning crusting of the eyelids but specifies it is not thick/purulent. Mild eyelid edema is present. The patient acknowledges a preceding upper respiratory tract infection (sore throat and runny nose) 3 days ago. Denies severe deep ocular pain, photophobia, or seeing halos around lights.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently impaired by blurred vision secondary to tearing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High risk of spreading the infection to work colleagues if not appropriately triaged.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 124/80 mmHg | HR: 76 bpm | Temp: 37.2 °C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Frequently dabbing at eyes with a tissue.
Recent/Relevant Labs: Fluorescein staining performed on-site is negative for corneal dendrites or ulceration.
Physical Exam: Bilateral ocular exam reveals diffuse conjunctival hyperemia and profuse watery discharge. A distinct follicular reaction is visible on the inferior palpebral conjunctiva. Mild eyelid edema is present. Preauricular lymph nodes are enlarged and tender to palpation bilaterally. Crucially, visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes once cleared of discharge. Pupils are equal and reactive. Corneas are clear. No ciliary flush noted.
Environmental Observations: Tissue waste container is overflowing; patient needs education on hygiene.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has used generic saline drops with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Viral Conjunctivitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Bacterial Conjunctivitis - Ruled out by the profuse watery (rather than thick, opaque purulent) discharge and the presence of preauricular lymphadenopathy.
Allergic Conjunctivitis - Unlikely given the preceding URI and the prominence of grittiness over intense pruritus (itching), although some overlap is possible.
Acute Keratitis (HSV/Bacterial) - Ruled out definitively by negative fluorescein staining and normal visual acuity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute bilateral presentation of "pink eye" with blurred vision necessitated an urgent home evaluation to exclude sight-threatening emergencies such as keratitis or glaucoma. Direct physical examination, including fluorescein staining, confirmed a benign but highly contagious viral conjunctivitis linked to the patient's recent URI. By definitively ruling out serious pathology and establishing strict quarantine and hygiene protocols in the home, an unnecessary and potentially infectious visit to an emergency department was averted. This decisive triage and infection control satisfy MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Advised regular use of lubricant eye drops (preservative-free) every 2 hours for symptomatic relief of grittiness. Recommended cool compresses 4 times daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically prevent the virus from spreading to your friends or family, you must treat your hands as if they are contaminated at all times. Use a fresh paper towel for every single dab of your eye and immediately discard it in a lined bin; using a reusable cloth handkerchief simply stores the live virus and ensures you reinfect yourself every time you use it.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Provided a supply of preservative-free lubricant drops.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 3-5 days if symptoms fail to improve or if discharge becomes purulent.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop severe, deep eye pain, sensitivity to light (photophobia), see halos around lights, or if your vision becomes persistently blurred despite clearing away the discharge.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; viral conjunctivitis is self-limiting but highly contagious for up to 12 days.
Clinical Course: Symptoms typically peak between days 3 and 5 and can take 2-3 weeks to resolve completely. The second eye often becomes involved shortly after the first.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if the discharge changes from watery to thick, yellow or green pus.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: This is basically a "cold" in your eyes caused by the same virus as your recent sore throat. Antibiotics will not work. The goal is to keep the eyes lubricated and clean while your immune system clears the virus.
Actionable Steps: Do not wear contact lenses until the eyes are perfectly white for 48 hours. Wash all pillowcases and towels in hot water. Avoid sharing any personal items.

SMS INFORMATION
Viral Conjunctivitis (Pink Eye). Highly contagious. Use lubricant drops for grittiness. Wash hands and use paper tissues only.
SMS EDUCATION
